WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.730
Spectrum, from 2024

2
00:00:01.802 --> 00:00:04.090
runs smart-contract transactions

3
00:00:04.161 --> 00:00:06.735
in parallel, but commits them in the

4
00:00:06.807 --> 00:00:08.380
order consensus fixed.

5
00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:09.587
On a conflict

6
00:00:09.649 --> 00:00:12.282
it rolls the later one back to a checkpoint

7
00:00:12.343 --> 00:00:13.690
taken partway through.

8
00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:16.308
A checkpoint saves the code position

9
00:00:16.371 --> 00:00:17.758
and the operand stack

10
00:00:17.821 --> 00:00:19.522
and copying a deep stack at

11
00:00:19.585 --> 00:00:21.160
every checkpoint is slow.

12
00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:24.220
So keep the stack in pages of four.

13
00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:27.091
A checkpoint keeps only the page table

14
00:00:27.156 --> 00:00:29.289
and a page is copied on its first

15
00:00:29.354 --> 00:00:30.970
write after a checkpoint.

16
00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:33.880
In our model, with sixteen checkpoints of

17
00:00:33.942 --> 00:00:35.503
a two-hundred-item stack

18
00:00:35.566 --> 00:00:37.814
copying the whole stack copies three

19
00:00:37.876 --> 00:00:40.061
thousand two hundred items to save

20
00:00:40.061 --> 00:00:41.560
and as many to restore.

21
00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:43.758
The paged stack copies none

22
00:00:43.827 --> 00:00:45.127
to save or restore

23
00:00:45.196 --> 00:00:47.112
and one thousand two hundred

24
00:00:47.181 --> 00:00:48.550
fifty-two on writes.

25
00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:51.324
Claude Opus 5.5 built this design

26
00:00:51.324 --> 00:00:53.606
and was one of only two passes

27
00:00:53.606 --> 00:00:55.959
a pass needs wins on throughput

28
00:00:55.959 --> 00:00:58.740
creation and restore time, and memory.

29
00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:00.950
Seer, also from 2024

30
00:01:01.027 --> 00:01:03.123
predicts which way contract

31
00:01:03.201 --> 00:01:04.520
branches will go.

32
00:01:04.870 --> 00:01:07.304
Each branch gets a small predictor

33
00:01:07.375 --> 00:01:10.310
that answers taken, not taken, or unsure.

34
00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:13.065
Claude Opus 5.5 predicted the last

35
00:01:13.136 --> 00:01:15.399
direction only after it had held

36
00:01:15.470 --> 00:01:17.380
across two distinct blocks.

37
00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:19.659
On our trace of sixty branches

38
00:01:19.721 --> 00:01:21.526
that halves the wrong guesses

39
00:01:21.588 --> 00:01:23.268
of the last-direction rule

40
00:01:23.268 --> 00:01:25.570
at the price of more unsure answers.

41
00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:27.002
On the real task

42
00:01:27.002 --> 00:01:29.611
ten runs reached the same rounded means

43
00:01:29.611 --> 00:01:31.520
and the pass rule split them.

44
00:01:31.870 --> 00:01:34.290
Kimi K3 passed with eighty-one

45
00:01:34.368 --> 00:01:36.632
while GPT-6 Astra failed with

46
00:01:36.710 --> 00:01:38.350
eighty-one point two.

47
00:01:38.930 --> 00:01:41.401
The last task is a persistent treap

48
00:01:41.401 --> 00:01:42.706
from a 2025 paper

49
00:01:42.774 --> 00:01:45.520
an ordered index where every transaction

50
00:01:45.589 --> 00:01:46.962
makes a new version

51
00:01:46.962 --> 00:01:49.570
and every old version stays readable.

52
00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:52.656
An update copies only the path it changes

53
00:01:52.656 --> 00:01:54.480
and shares everything else.

54
00:01:54.830 --> 00:01:55.655
In our model

55
00:01:55.719 --> 00:01:58.194
about a dozen new nodes per update keep

56
00:01:58.258 --> 00:02:00.670
three thousand and one versions alive.

57
00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:04.850
Claude Opus 5.5 made the updates parallel.

58
00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:07.263
Nodes with a high priority form

59
00:02:07.330 --> 00:02:08.727
the top of the treap

60
00:02:08.727 --> 00:02:11.323
and each subtree below them is a unit

61
00:02:11.323 --> 00:02:12.720
owned by one worker.

62
00:02:13.070 --> 00:02:15.758
Workers copy paths inside their own units

63
00:02:15.822 --> 00:02:18.702
and a linker thread copies the short top path

64
00:02:18.766 --> 00:02:21.070
and publishes each version in order.

65
00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:23.820
Twelve of sixteen models passed.

66
00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:25.830
Opus scored forty point two

67
00:02:25.889 --> 00:02:28.438
about four point seven times the throughput

68
00:02:28.498 --> 00:02:29.742
of a serial scaffold

69
00:02:29.742 --> 00:02:32.410
where the reference reaches one point three.

70
00:02:32.990 --> 00:02:35.710
That ends the lecture, with three lessons.

71
00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:37.636
Do the cheap pass first

72
00:02:37.636 --> 00:02:39.343
drop rows before joining

73
00:02:39.409 --> 00:02:41.575
and stop probing when more probes

74
00:02:41.641 --> 00:02:43.020
are unlikely to help.

75
00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:46.044
Reorganize the data, not just the loop

76
00:02:46.044 --> 00:02:49.130
as the best JoinBoost and Spectrum runs did.

77
00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:52.282
And the pass rule decides: on Yannakakis

78
00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:54.263
thirty-nine strict wins were

79
00:02:54.332 --> 00:02:56.040
too many for every model.

80
00:02:56.390 --> 00:02:59.167
As an exercise, use pages of sixteen items

81
00:02:59.233 --> 00:03:00.490
in the stack model

82
00:03:00.490 --> 00:03:02.870
and see what happens to the copies.

83
00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:07.060
Next time: Lecture 9, programming languages.
